// magnitudes: exponential above mMin - dM/2, then binned to 0.1
const beta = b * Math.LN10;
m = Math.round((mMin - 0.05 + -Math.log(1 - r()) / beta) * 10) / 10;
// Omori-Utsu times on [0, T]
const a = c ** (1 - p), z = (T + c) ** (1 - p);
t = (a - r() * (a - z)) ** (1 / (1 - p)) - c;
```
-ln(1 - U) / beta is exponential with rate beta. Starting at mMin - 0.05 and rounding makes the lowest bin exactly mMin, which is the setup Utsu's correction assumes. For times, the CDF on [0, T] is F(t) = ((t + c)^(1-p) - c^(1-p)) / ((T + c)^(1-p) - c^(1-p)), and solving F(t) = U gives the second line. r is mulberry32, a 32 bit seeded generator, so every test is deterministic. Fitting a model to data drawn from that same model catches implementation bugs; it cannot catch model misspecification on real data.
